# LAB 5 — Reverse Engineering : OWASP UnCrackable Level 2

---

## Overview

This lab analyzes an Android application that hides its validation logic inside a **native library**. The app looks simple — a text field and a button — but the real check happens in compiled C/C++ code, not in Java. The goal is to trace the input from the UI all the way to the native comparison and extract the secret string.

**Verification flow:**

```
User input
   ↓
MainActivity
   ↓
CodeCheck.a(...)
   ↓
CodeCheck.bar(...)      ← native method
   ↓
libfoo.so
   ↓
strncmp(input, secret)
   ↓
success / failure
```

**Expected secret:** `Thanks for all the fish`

---

## Learning Objectives

By the end of this lab you will be able to:

- Understand the role of an APK in Android
- Observe app behavior before reading any code
- Identify the validation entry point in `MainActivity`
- Recognize native library loading via `System.loadLibrary`
- Understand what a `native` method declaration means in Java
- Locate a `.so` file inside an APK
- Open a native library in Ghidra
- Find an exported JNI function
- Spot a `strncmp` string comparison in decompiled code
- Decode a hex ASCII value
- Retrieve and validate the final secret

