## Application Flow Summary

```
User types input
       ↓
MainActivity reads the EditText value
       ↓
Calls CodeCheck.a(input)
       ↓
CodeCheck.a() calls native method bar(input)
       ↓
JNI bridges to libfoo.so
       ↓
Java_..._CodeCheck_bar runs in native code
       ↓
strncmp(input, "Thanks for all the fish")
       ↓
returns true → success   /   false → error message
```

---

## Key Takeaways

Sensitive logic can be moved from Java into native code. This makes analysis harder but not impossible. The method stays the same:

1. Observe behavior before reading code
2. Follow the user input through the Java layer
3. Identify the class that bridges to native (`CodeCheck`)
4. Spot the `System.loadLibrary` call and the `native` method
5. Extract the `.so` from the APK
6. Open it in Ghidra and run auto-analysis
7. Find the exported JNI function (`Java_..._bar`)
8. Locate the `strncmp` call in the decompiled pseudo-code
9. Extract and decode the reference string
10. Validate in the app

---

## Troubleshooting

| Problem | Solution |
|---|---|
| JADX does not show the right class quickly | Use global search for `MainActivity`, `CodeCheck`, or `loadLibrary` |
| Ghidra shows too many functions | Filter the Symbol Tree with `Java_` or `CodeCheck_bar` |
| The value found looks unreadable | Check if it is hex ASCII — convert with `bytes.fromhex()` |
| Decoded string still looks wrong | Try reversing it with `s[::-1]` |
| App still rejects the answer | Check exact capitalization, spaces, and spelling |

---

## Conclusion

UnCrackable Level 2 demonstrates a classic technique in Android obfuscation: moving the critical validation from readable Java bytecode into a compiled native library. The Java layer only delegates — the actual secret never appears in any `.class` or `.dex` file. By following the data flow from `MainActivity` through `CodeCheck` into `libfoo.so`, and using Ghidra to decompile the JNI function, the `strncmp` comparison reveals a reversed hex-encoded string that decodes to `Thanks for all the fish`.
